sections:
- intended use
- how to use
- available parameters
- warnings that can be raised by this map



intended use
------------

This map is intended for use with amide (~N(D)C(=O)~) (smiles notation) groups, specifically those that are found in the protein backbone. In other words, it looks for the amide units formed through linking amino acids.

The goal is to calculate the absorption frequency of the Amide I mode, which is dominated by the C=O stretch.

Strictly speaking, amide I' is used for the deuterated vibrations as found when the protein is solvated in heave water. Amide I is used for the vibration with hygrogenated nitrogen as found when the protein is solvated in H2O. The maps in this file perform the amide I' calculation (so the deuterated form). If non-deuterated values are desired, the resulting spectrum should be blue-shifted by about 10 cm-1. (https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0064518)


how to use
----------

When you want to calculate a full hamiltonian for a protein, you'll most likely want to use this map in combination with two others. Besides this map, consider using the AmideSC map (for amide groups in the side chains), and the ProteinAmide map (for calculating couplings between protein amides). Please note that while the AmideBB and AmideSC maps are singles maps, the ProteinAmide is a pairs map.

Be aware that the maps used in this overarching map have been designed to be used in combination with specific other maps, or with specific force fields. While a map should still work with a different forcefield, it is still highly recommended to use the intended/tested combinations or validate the alternative choice before using it. 
- In general, the OPLS-AA forcefield is recommended, except for the Tokmakoff map, which has been designed for the CHARMM-27 forcefield.
- The Jansen dipole map should always be used together with the Jansen frequency map (this is enforced by this map).
- It is common to also calculate couplings when working with amides. This should be done with one of the specially-designed amide coupling maps. See (https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jctc.6b00420) to find out what coupling map is best for your usecase.
- The peaks for these systems take up the same spectral space as sidechain amides, so to get better agreement with experiment, you'll probably want to use the sidechain map along this one.
- The map has been confirmed to work with GROMACs, CHARMM, NAMD and Amber. If you use the map on MD files from any software with GROMACS (or just any system with non-cannonical amino acids), make sure to use the parameter AmideBB.include_protein_residues (more information below) in your runs.



Available parameters
--------------------

When using this map, the following parameters become available to use in any input parameter source (default file, input file, command line):

AmideBB.frequency_map_choice
    shorthand: AmideBB.fmc
    options: Tokmakoff, Skinner, Jansen, Cho, Hirst, Reppert_4PN-4, Reppert_4PN-150 (default = Skinner)

    Indicate the program which frequency map should be used. Skinner is the default as it gives the best result for 2-dimensional IR absorption spectra, while being fairly cheap. It is also the only map which has a separately-developed map for sidechains, instead of using the backbone maps with a fixed shift.  (https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jctc.6b00420)

    Tokmakoff, Cho, Hirst and Reppert maps are cheaper than Skinner, but still provide decent results. Jansen is more expensive than Skinner, but gives results closer to experiment when predicting linear IR spectra. (same reference)

    Similar to how Skinner is the only map with a special sidechain map, the Jansen map is the only map with a special variant for amide groups with a proline residue on the C-terminal side (called a 'prepro(line)' in GMAP). All other mappings use a fixed shift on the 'general' amide map to account for the prepro shift. (https://doi.org/10.1063/1.3665417)

AmideBB.dipole_map_choice
    shorthand: AmideBB.dmc
    options: Torii, Jansen (default = Torii)

    Indicates which dipole map should be used. Torii is the default as the Skinner frequency map assumes it, and is more general. Jansen should only be used when using the Jansen frequency map, too.

AmideBB.include_protein_residues
    no shorthand
    default: ARN ASH GLH HID HIE HIP HSD LYN LYSH

    To turn this off, use 'None' (without the quotation marks) as the only choice.

    Which residue names exist in the simulation that are a part of the protein, but not cannonical amino acids. Any residues mentioned here are expected to contain the amide-backbone of N, CA, and C atoms. If a middle-chain residue, it also must have an 'O' atom (the double-bounded one to C), and H/HN (depending on software, HN for CHARMM, H for GROMACS/amber) atom on the nitrogen. A chain-ending residue needs only one of these two.

    If a special residue does not make these requirements, a separate line in the core.txt file needs to be created for it instead.

AmideBB.residue_order
    no shorthand
    options: resname, resnum (default = resnum)

    In what order the residues should be saved. This influences all output files (like hamiltonian, dipoles, etc). The difference is caused by the fact this map treats two different protein residues.

    resnum has been taken as the default, as this is the same order that AIM uses.

AmideBB.pos_choice
    no shorthand
    options: C, O, N, D, Torii (default = C)

    When saving the position of this group, what position should be taken. The first four options save the position of that atom, Torii saves the position at which the Torii dipoles are assumed. This is in between the CNO atoms: 0.077*C + 0.665*O + 0.258*N

AmideBB.dp1_choice
    no shorthand
    options: C, O, N, D, Torii (default = C)

    When saving the doublepos of this group, what position should be taken as first position. The first four options save the position of that atom, Torii saves the position at which the Torii dipoles are assumed. This is in between the CNO atoms: 0.077*C + 0.665*O + 0.258*N

AmideBB.dp2_choice
    no shorthand
    options: C, O, N, D, Torii (default = N)

    When saving the doublepos of this group, what position should be taken as second position. The first four options save the position of that atom, Torii saves the position at which the Torii dipoles are assumed. This is in between the CNO atoms: 0.077*C + 0.665*O + 0.258*N

AmideBB.consider_nearest_neighbours
    shorthand: AmideBB.cnn
    options: True, False (default = True)

    When calculating the electrostatic properties, the environment contains charges that are allowed to influence those properties. But how do we treat charges very close by? The system is a protein, with small residues, so there are very similar charges very close to the treated oscillator.
    If this parameter is set to true, the neighbouring residues are excluded from the standard electrostatics calculation, and treated with specially designed maps instead. These maps add a shift to the calculated frequency based on the ramachandran angles between the different residues.

    !!! IMPORTANT !!!
    This setting changes how the electrostatic properties are calculated, and therefore has an influence on all electrostatics-dependent properties (Jansen dipoles included).

    The Tokmakoff map is not allowed to be influenced by this setting, it never uses this special extra map. The Jansen maps (both frequency and dipole) are designed with it in mind, so give less accurate results if this is turned off.

AmideBB.legacy_mode
    no shorthand
    options: None, AIM, AmideImaps (default = None)

    Whether to recreate the results as produced by the precursors of this package. All options create results of the same format and magnitude, making all of them valid for use with software like NISE.
    
    These are the differences:

    - All positions lie between 0 and the box size for AIM, while None and AmideImaps have them lie in the range set using the GMAP parameter treat_box. By default, this is now also between 0 and the box size.
    - When there are two prolines back to back, what map is used changes a little bit between modes. AmideImaps assigned a Gly-Pro map for those cases, while AIM and None assign a Pro-Gly map.

    Which should you use? If you just want to compute spectra, stick with 'none'. It gives the most recent, detailed version, with any known bugs fixed. The main reason for changing to a legacy mode is for re-creating results from previous versions, so comparison between different results or packages can be made.

    Warning!
    When using the legacy_mode set to AIM, the treat_box parameter is ignored by this map!

AmideBB.solvent
    no shorthand
    options: D2O, H2O (default = D2O)

    What the solvent of the system should be. The calculation assumes that the hydrogen atom on the nitrogen of the amide bond is of the same type as the solvent. However, the change of weight of the atom is not considered - it's movement and weight are determined by the preceeding MD simulation. This parameter influences whether the computed frequencies will be shifted.

AmideBB.shift_base
    no shorthand
    options: see text

    What kind of shift should be applied to the frequencies computed for AmideBB groups to account for the isotopes present. This shift will be applied to all groups not specifically selected using the parameter AmideBB.labels.
    You can either provide a decimal number of choice, or pick one of the following presets (that each come with their corresponding reference in program output):

    'C12', 'C12_O16' and 'natural' all mean to assume the same isotopes as in nature. No shift will be applied, as the maps used all assume these isotopes.

    'C13' and 'C13_O16' are used for C13-labels, for which a redshift of 45 wavenumbers is assumed.

    'C13_O18' is used for a label with both heavy carbon and oxygen, for which a redshift of 59.6 wavenumbers is assumed.

AmideBB.shift_label
    no shorthand
    options: same as AmideBB.shift_base

    What kind of shift should be applied to the frequencies computed for AmideBB groups to account for the isotopes present. This shift will be applied to all groups that are specifically selected using the parameter AmideBB.labels.

AmideBB.labels
    no shorthand
    options: None, resnum (+ choices), resname (+ choices) (default = None)

    Which aminoacids in the protein should be isotope labelled. By default, no labels are used/assumed. This choice can also be explicitly specified by using 'None'.

    'resnum 2 5' states that the residues numbered 2 and 5 (zero-based) should be considered 'labelled' by the program. Similarly, 'resnum 2-5' states that the residues 2, 3, 4 and 5 (zero-based) should be considered 'labelled' by the program. As long as they are separated by spaces, as many numbers and ranges are allowed. So, the following input line is perfectly valid:
    'AmideBB.labels resnum 1 3-6 9 10 15-16 18-25 29 32'

    Similarly to 'resnum' the mode 'resname' specifies which residue names should be labelled. As many names can be given as wanted, as long as they are either the cannonical 20 3-letter codes, or any option chosen for the parameter AmideBB.include_protein_residues.

AmideBB.allow_map_mismatch
    no shorthand
    options: true, false (default = false)

    When using this map in conjunction with the AmideSC map (developed for protein sidechain amides), it makes most physical sense to use the same frequency and dipole methods for both maps. By default, this map forces users to use the same methods for both maps (in order to avoid user error). If it is truly intended to have different methods active, this parameter can be used to have the map allow that.

AmideBB.Torii_dipole_angle
    no shorthand
    options: any float (default = 10)

    When using the Torii dipole method, the angle at which the dipole is placed can be varied by changing this parameter. The value is assumed in degrees angle to the C=O bond.



warnings that can be raised by this map
---------------------------------------

map_AmideBB_0
    This error is raised when the map couldn't be used correctly, either due to a bug, or due to broken files. If the problem persists, even with a freshly re-downloaded and re-installed map, please contact the developers!

map_AmideBB_1
    This error is raised when the Jansen dipole map is requested without the
    Jansen frequency map. The Jansen dipole map may be used if and only if the Jansen frequency map is used in the same run.

    This has to do with the fact that both maps require knowledge of the electrostatic gradient, and no other frequency map does. If any other frequency map is used, no gradient data will be collected, so results from this map will be wrong.

    Another reason is the fact that the two maps have been developed together with the intention of them working together. That means that to obtain the best results with either one, the other is needed, too.

map_AmideBB_2
    This error is raised when the Jansen dipole map is requested without the
    Jansen frequency map. The Jansen dipole map may be used if and only if the Jansen frequency map is used in the same run.

    This has to do with the fact that both maps require knowledge of the electrostatic gradient, and no other frequency map does. If any other frequency map is used, no gradient data will be collected, so results from this map will be wrong.

    Another reason is the fact that the two maps have been developed together with the intention of them working together. That means that to obtain the best results with either one, the other is needed, too.


map_AmideBB_3
    This error is raised when any of the neighbour influence maps has the wrong dimensions, or anything else is wrong with the file.

map_AmideBB_4
    While determining the chirality of residues between two amide oscillators, a residue was found that is not a GLY residue, but neither has a CB atom. Therefore, this cannot be a cannonical aminoacid, or you are using an unexpected nomenclature. If you are sure you are applying the right map to the right system, please get in touch with the map developers to solve this issue.

map_AmideBB_5
    There is only a few valid options for this parameter, listed higher up in this file. Make sure you match the name, capital letters included, apostrophes excluded. Otherwise, when specifying a decimal number, make sure you only use numbers and a period ('.') to provide this.

map_AmideBB_6
    Some issue was encountered when parsing the choice for the parameter AmideBB.labels. Unless your choice is 'None' (mind the capitalization!), your choice should specify both a mode (resnums or resnames currently) and a selection using that mode. If there are multiple parts to your selection, they should be separated using spaces just as in the examples.
